1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,808
Aarav charges his phone
with a long, thin cable.

2
00:00:03,808 --> 00:00:05,165
It charges slowly.

3
00:00:05,515 --> 00:00:08,321
A short, thick cable charges it faster.

4
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:09,374
Same charger.

5
00:00:09,374 --> 00:00:11,715
So what is the thin cable doing?

6
00:00:12,215 --> 00:00:14,085
It is resisting the current.

7
00:00:14,085 --> 00:00:17,777
Every material pushes back a
little on the moving electrons.

8
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:22,351
Inside a wire, electrons keep
bumping into the vibrating atoms.

9
00:00:22,351 --> 00:00:24,256
Each bump slows them down.

10
00:00:24,606 --> 00:00:28,512
This opposition to the flow of
current is called resistance, R.

11
00:00:29,162 --> 00:00:32,225
So how are voltage, current
and resistance linked?

12
00:00:32,225 --> 00:00:34,212
Let's test it on the breadboard.

13
00:00:34,562 --> 00:00:37,917
A variable supply, an
ammeter, and one resistor.

14
00:00:37,917 --> 00:00:40,396
A voltmeter sits across the resistor.

15
00:00:40,746 --> 00:00:42,813
Set the supply to two volts.

16
00:00:42,813 --> 00:00:45,506
The current is zero point five amperes.

17
00:00:45,856 --> 00:00:47,669
Four volts, one ampere.

18
00:00:47,669 --> 00:00:50,180
Six volts, one point five amperes.

19
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,098
Eight volts, two amperes.

20
00:00:52,448 --> 00:00:53,638
Plot each reading.

21
00:00:53,638 --> 00:00:56,493
The points fall on a
straight line through zero.

22
00:00:57,143 --> 00:01:00,257
Now swap in a resistor
twice as big, and repeat.

23
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:03,370
At every voltage, the current is now half.

24
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:05,464
The new line is twice as steep.

25
00:01:05,814 --> 00:01:10,188
On a graph of V against I, the
slope of the line is the resistance.

26
00:01:10,838 --> 00:01:12,211
This is Ohm's law.

27
00:01:12,211 --> 00:01:17,251
Georg Simon Ohm, a German physicist,
published it in eighteen twenty seven.

28
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:22,129
At a steady temperature, the current
through a conductor is directly proportional

29
00:01:22,129 --> 00:01:23,786
to the voltage across it.

30
00:01:24,486 --> 00:01:26,555
So V is proportional to I.

31
00:01:26,555 --> 00:01:30,194
Put in a constant, R,
and V equals I times R.

32
00:01:30,894 --> 00:01:34,483
Turn it around, and R
equals V divided by I.

33
00:01:35,183 --> 00:01:39,606
Resistance is measured in ohms,
written with the Greek letter omega.

34
00:01:39,606 --> 00:01:41,832
One ohm is one volt per ampere.

35
00:01:42,482 --> 00:01:45,352
Metals obey this law at
a steady temperature.

36
00:01:45,352 --> 00:01:47,778
Some parts, like a diode, do not.

37
00:01:47,778 --> 00:01:49,415
We call them non ohmic.

38
00:01:50,065 --> 00:01:52,353
Double the push, double the flow.

39
00:01:52,353 --> 00:01:54,088
Resistance stays the same.

40
00:01:54,888 --> 00:01:57,679
So what decides the resistance of a wire?

41
00:01:57,679 --> 00:01:58,703
Three things.

42
00:01:59,053 --> 00:01:59,953
Length.

43
00:01:59,703 --> 00:02:01,833
A longer wire means more bumps.

44
00:02:01,833 --> 00:02:04,318
R is proportional to the length, L.

45
00:02:05,018 --> 00:02:05,918
Thickness.

46
00:02:05,876 --> 00:02:08,111
A wider wire gives more paths.

47
00:02:08,111 --> 00:02:12,358
R is inversely proportional to
the area of cross section, A.

48
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:14,389
And the material.

49
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:15,772
Put them together.

50
00:02:15,772 --> 00:02:18,455
R equals rho times L, divided by A.

51
00:02:19,155 --> 00:02:22,871
Rho is the resistivity of
the material, in ohm metres.

52
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:27,166
Copper has a very low resistivity,
so wires are made of copper.

53
00:02:27,816 --> 00:02:30,932
That is why the long,
thin cable charges slowly.

54
00:02:30,932 --> 00:02:33,594
More length, less area, more resistance.

55
00:02:34,244 --> 00:02:35,423
Worked example.

56
00:02:35,423 --> 00:02:40,846
A resistor has six volts across it, and
a current of zero point three amperes.

57
00:02:41,196 --> 00:02:42,813
R equals V by I.

58
00:02:42,813 --> 00:02:46,573
Six divided by zero point
three is twenty ohms.

59
00:02:46,923 --> 00:02:49,864
Now raise the voltage to nine volts.

60
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:51,471
I equals V by R.

61
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:55,757
Nine divided by twenty is
zero point four five amperes.

62
00:02:56,407 --> 00:02:57,870
Now, the exam trap.

63
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:03,019
Many students read R equals V by I,
and think a bigger voltage makes a

64
00:03:03,019 --> 00:03:04,358
bigger resistance.

65
00:03:04,708 --> 00:03:05,659
It does not.

66
00:03:05,659 --> 00:03:09,875
R depends on the material, the
length, the area and the temperature.

67
00:03:09,875 --> 00:03:11,545
V by I only measures it.

68
00:03:12,195 --> 00:03:13,937
Where is this asked?

69
00:03:13,937 --> 00:03:15,678
In PUC board papers.

70
00:03:15,678 --> 00:03:20,703
State Ohm's law, define resistance
and resistivity, and solve problems

71
00:03:20,703 --> 00:03:24,290
with V equals IR and R equals rho L by A.

72
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:25,840
Try this.

73
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:30,744
A wire of resistance ten ohms is stretched
evenly to three times its length.

74
00:03:30,744 --> 00:03:33,631
It is then connected to a nine volt cell.

75
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:35,034
What current flows?

76
00:03:40,434 --> 00:03:41,334
C.

77
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:43,017
Zero point one amperes.

78
00:03:43,367 --> 00:03:45,638
Stretching keeps the volume the same.

79
00:03:45,638 --> 00:03:48,775
Three times the length
means one third of the area.

80
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:52,789
So R becomes three times
three, nine times bigger.

81
00:03:52,789 --> 00:03:53,837
Ninety ohms.

82
00:03:54,187 --> 00:03:56,163
Then I equals V by R.

83
00:03:56,163 --> 00:04:00,020
Nine divided by ninety is
zero point one amperes.

84
00:04:00,671 --> 00:04:02,192
Screenshot this card.

85
00:04:05,392 --> 00:04:07,573
Next, series and parallel circuits.

86
00:04:07,573 --> 00:04:11,337
